#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166417 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "ac2a2137-53fa-5121-b541-02e0addd8757" :question/math-academy-id "e-12356" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n},$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle x,\\: 2z,\\: z-y \\right\\rangle\\!, \n\\quad \n\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle z,\\: -y,\\: -x \\right\\rangle\\!,\n\\quad\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}}{\\Vert \\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}} \\Vert}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/assets/35bcfa2242051bed034ac3343c1a6d67f94916d4267030f726e2bd27cbbe02f1.png)\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{(z)^{2}+(-y)^{2}+(-x)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{z}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,2z,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,2z,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot ⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xz-2yz-x(z-y)}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-2yz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{xy-2yz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166418 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Horizontal Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "fba5ed01-4c4c-50f6-8060-76bf40df79b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12384" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle z,\\: z,\\: 4xy\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=1$ for $0\\leq x\\leq 1$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1.$ Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for  $0\\leq x\\leq 1$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, -1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166419 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "35c3c9ba-60b0-5bcf-8e9c-2862ece44e7e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12454" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle y,\\: 0,\\: z\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x+2y$ for $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1.$ Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||\\mathbf r_x \\times \\mathbf r_y|| =3.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z = 2x+2y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n\\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle -2,\\: -2, \\: 1\\rangle}_{\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}} = 0, \\quad \\textrm{or}\\quad \\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle 2,\\: 2, \\: -1\\rangle}_{-\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}}= 0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{\\mathbf n} = \\langle -2,\\, -2,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x+2y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,0,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨y,\\,0,\\,2x+2y⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2y+0x+2x+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{2}{3}x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{3}x\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{3}x\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\cancel{3}}x\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[y]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [{x}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0f632e1-bc4a-5e20-9d42-993ea0ac140f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2} + 2yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163803} {:answer/id #uuid "d9597573-13cf-5b29-80fc-a0cec25c3d02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2} + yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163804} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf7721d-433b-562f-8b02-6dc0eaf18ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy - yz + 2xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163805} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a59231-39be-5bd9-b000-7fd5bea7b50b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy + yz + xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163806} {:answer/id #uuid "0cfc1e63-d79d-5c0a-a85e-bb0f5321e73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2} + 3yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9597573-13cf-5b29-80fc-a0cec25c3d02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2} + yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163804} :answer-field/id #uuid "800fbb75-73ff-5c83-b570-3144244101d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7492e5fe-6cc9-5d4b-8d1f-20d212147cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-160838" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{\\boxed{}}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{(2y)^{2}+(-z)^{2}+(2x)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2yz-yz+2{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+yz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}+yz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Horizontal Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4af988d5-6e7a-5e67-a1fa-1319abb9d6ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354136} {:answer/id #uuid "2b9edefe-b7e0-59a8-94d4-440c23f40581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354137} {:answer/id #uuid "31cae73c-16a9-5852-990d-c999f077afab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354138} {:answer/id #uuid "c2430f16-7d47-5eaa-8db5-0128a0089d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354139} {:answer/id #uuid "5ece327e-4ce6-5f80-bfd8-084166c394db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ece327e-4ce6-5f80-bfd8-084166c394db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354140} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e9d31df-64fd-5993-9d88-c8494001bc99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0fb338bb-c8d9-50c5-addd-465e82c29978" :question/math-academy-id "q-161192" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨yz,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=4$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=4$ for  $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨yz,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4y,\\,4x,\\,xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}xy\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{-1}^{1}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6915ef1-f87c-5002-b38e-c005c3880616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354143} {:answer/id #uuid "8e23539d-d6fa-5b4d-99f5-6819d39d73c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354144} {:answer/id #uuid "c01105be-2fe8-55f9-875a-39151c2fdfdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354145} {:answer/id #uuid "be456f7b-7401-5b12-8145-b88431667184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354146} {:answer/id #uuid "04e037c9-6da9-55dd-beb3-9501ffb6606a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04e037c9-6da9-55dd-beb3-9501ffb6606a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354147} :answer-field/id #uuid "e028a01c-5683-5d1d-a1e0-a752115fb27c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8620aa7-548d-5834-a6eb-0fd02b29a086" :question/math-academy-id "q-161194" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨-2y,\\,\\frac{x}{2},\\,z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2y-x-5$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 0.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{6}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2y-x-5$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-5,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=5.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2y-x-5$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨-2y,\\,\\frac{x}{2},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-2y,\\,\\frac{x}{2},\\,2y-x-5⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-2y-x+2y-x-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-2x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-2x-5)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+5)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+5)\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}}(2x+5)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-[y]_{-1}^{0}\\cdot [{x}^{2}+5x]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-(0+1)\\cdot (1+5-0) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0484fd1e-871f-59fa-b962-ccdb195cad3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y-x-2{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354150} {:answer/id #uuid "ab23ec92-0f8f-5ad0-8806-583585df8df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354151} {:answer/id #uuid "86306ce9-20a4-5730-8c71-b15f99ca3bc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354152} {:answer/id #uuid "f49e49b4-b900-53d6-b790-a294ca471046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354153} {:answer/id #uuid "15b63f7e-dcca-5164-973e-fbc764e968f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f49e49b4-b900-53d6-b790-a294ca471046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354153} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcc8ddbe-f5cf-597d-adae-a75ffe46c214" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd68aee4-128c-55a1-8849-b847e40933c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161198" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{z}^{2},\\,x,\\,y-{z}^{2}⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨{z}^{2},\\,x,\\,y-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨{z}^{2},\\,x,\\,y-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2{z}^{2}-x+2(y-{z}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y-x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{2y-x}{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{S}2y-x\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6f89fb0-f44e-5ceb-b0fa-cfdbd042c78d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354157} {:answer/id #uuid "4c759f56-6454-5deb-b9d0-34e63fc3e836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy-3{e}^{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354158} {:answer/id #uuid "0ee3964b-5ca7-5728-ae0a-437b77f78445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy+{e}^{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354159} {:answer/id #uuid "9445a197-d84d-5878-8572-b036b048897c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy-3-{e}^{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354160} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0ee45a-cf61-5277-be9a-e5b01c0e52ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{z}+xy+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c759f56-6454-5deb-b9d0-34e63fc3e836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy-3{e}^{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354158} :answer-field/id #uuid "13da6767-91ad-5eca-8a6f-7a354735d465" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50e3bbd7-ac94-517f-8378-1fb0a3b15c82" :question/math-academy-id "q-161199" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨1-{e}^{z},\\,xy,\\,-3⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨1-{e}^{z},\\,xy,\\,-3⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨1-{e}^{z},\\,xy,\\,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(3(1-{e}^{z})+xy-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-3{e}^{z}}{\\sqrt{11}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{xy-3{e}^{z}}{\\sqrt{11}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}{∬}_{S}xy-3{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b1b160a-37ba-5665-82ec-24ee8680c3ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy-yz+2xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354164} {:answer/id #uuid "39872af4-30af-5a84-b1da-d87b13553dc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3xz-yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354165} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a94b08-d9ac-5170-b99f-02cdba39f6f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354166} {:answer/id #uuid "ca57acbc-6787-50f9-80ff-ecdba16668fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy+yz+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354167} {:answer/id #uuid "bf014fd6-fac3-57a8-8adf-1f320e7d21d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy-2yz+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b1b160a-37ba-5665-82ec-24ee8680c3ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy-yz+2xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354164} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b584cb0-388b-57c3-9ae4-64c65eedac7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9cf65a4b-2e8e-5496-9a1a-b1343d6ea749" :question/math-academy-id "q-161200" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨xy,\\,yz,\\,xz⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,2⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨xy,\\,yz,\\,xz⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨xy,\\,yz,\\,xz⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(xy-yz+2xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-yz+2xz}{\\sqrt{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{xy-yz+2xz}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{∬}_{S}xy-yz+2xz\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51740d6a-b8d1-5862-969f-8c927d6c3abd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy+{z}^{2}-xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354171} {:answer/id #uuid "e1db64a4-9765-5556-9713-080d48773c1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}-xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354172} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f873db-34b8-50bb-8eef-00ec97802a32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy-{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354173} {:answer/id #uuid "01a9d1a8-72fb-53fe-af05-ef008e797544" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}-xyz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354174} {:answer/id #uuid "303d3e86-b7e9-5441-a01a-c4b28a14cd18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy+2{z}^{2}-xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1db64a4-9765-5556-9713-080d48773c1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}-xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354172} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e8b50ee-7eca-5889-99fe-1cdcdb9c946d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8d6736e-4bf0-5cd4-a023-43b5c372235a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161210" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\,x-z,\\,-2x⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,y⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{\\boxed{}}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}+(-z)^{2}+(y)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,x-z,\\,-2x⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨y,\\,x-z,\\,-2x⟩\\cdot ⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy-z(x-z)-2xy}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{z}^{2}-xz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{{z}^{2}-xz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78c42c5d-766a-52c2-8bbe-47f3f6306cd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354178} {:answer/id #uuid "e35aea69-ad41-5c21-8f1e-7c0fee78b343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354179} {:answer/id #uuid "10b9d577-e816-5b85-bbec-07fdc3b5bbe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354180} {:answer/id #uuid "84dc99df-bfaa-59f6-bd26-46bbe29439aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354181} {:answer/id #uuid "d8546ba5-f972-5922-9adf-123a2b9fde37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10b9d577-e816-5b85-bbec-07fdc3b5bbe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354180} :answer-field/id #uuid "61b02866-e594-5d23-bdf8-42482fcd61a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "712b23f7-2acb-542c-b6c5-1a7ef8fe14eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162147" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,z,\\,y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\frac{3}{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,z,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1,\\,y⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-x+(x-2y-2)+2y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{\\cancel{2}}{\\cancel{3}})\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{3}}{\\cancel{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f9e35f5-c3bb-5378-8b76-a0c4d847a0d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354185} {:answer/id #uuid "008a0b3b-5b2f-541a-bb2d-59af0bd86ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354186} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d5a3de-0a74-5caf-845a-fbdd89e04c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354187} {:answer/id #uuid "d3584d84-bea1-5249-929f-abcbd126cbac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354188} {:answer/id #uuid "d1dd8703-0ae3-50c7-9f7a-818d4125dafd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1dd8703-0ae3-50c7-9f7a-818d4125dafd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354189} :answer-field/id #uuid "4290f29c-4976-5702-a085-00b0852a2988" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "236061fb-0f20-5446-8754-3d5fb811ae32" :question/math-academy-id "q-162154" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,z-x+6⟩$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=x-y$ such that $\\text{Area}(S)=\\sqrt{6}.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x-y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x-y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,z-x+6⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,6-y⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-(x+3)+(x+y)+(6-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f4ffbb-505a-5d71-90b0-7212c9a96a83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354192} {:answer/id #uuid "00815175-e424-55ac-9a61-00315846fc49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354193} {:answer/id #uuid "5e1ac034-5dd4-5972-b196-64538f826278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354194} {:answer/id #uuid "dc64109e-4907-5b84-8276-84dbf454211e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354195} {:answer/id #uuid "6382e7b6-3bd6-57c6-a4ad-050d0d353fe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00815175-e424-55ac-9a61-00315846fc49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354193} :answer-field/id #uuid "36959d77-1a54-57f2-9b6d-b2116a11c76a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3200d2a8-1cae-51d8-8794-e7d6274b1d00" :question/math-academy-id "q-162540" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,-y,\\,4x+3⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=x+2y+1$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{6}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+2y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+2y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,-y,\\,4x+3⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨x+2y+1,\\,-y,\\,4x+3⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-x-2y-1+2y+4x+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}}(3x+2)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[y]_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+2x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef333620-18bc-5ec6-8edb-1abaacacd280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354199} {:answer/id #uuid "fc569fbc-647c-5e71-b7b7-77cb9fee5a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354200} {:answer/id #uuid "37e83d8a-fc75-52f0-a827-14063aeed473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354201} {:answer/id #uuid "cd26752f-37fc-53f0-82f7-8fee681c24f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354202} {:answer/id #uuid "ee18f982-7795-5f2b-aa91-977777b2f3dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd26752f-37fc-53f0-82f7-8fee681c24f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354202} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5fc0f3c-2f72-51df-b859-c3e9c4ae3146" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1d29d529-ae54-5c24-8758-72c09c8d1696" :question/math-academy-id "q-162546" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\,x,\\,z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=3x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{11}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=3x-y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-3,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3x-y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨y,\\,x,\\,3x-y⟩\\cdot ⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(3y-x-(3x-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x)\\cdot \\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{11}}}(4y-4x)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{11}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4y-4x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[4xy-2{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4y-2)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[2{y}^{2}-2y]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Horizontal Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7e6d076-25d7-5209-ab9d-6fcd2a3f375e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354206} {:answer/id #uuid "52cfb6e5-1e46-55a1-935f-d1d7e172bdb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354207} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf12ae2-6564-54dc-80bd-577d5c44c282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354208} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9a596f-577b-5d49-b924-e368b82996a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354209} {:answer/id #uuid "73bdeb0f-0f3a-5c4a-85b2-94a6a93e3dea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73bdeb0f-0f3a-5c4a-85b2-94a6a93e3dea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354210} :answer-field/id #uuid "6acdd215-c006-518c-a74b-a0fdadc54afe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9dd79803-39bc-570f-ac7b-87b2d6cbc27f" :question/math-academy-id "q-365878" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\,z,\\,2x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $0\\le y\\le 2,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,z,\\,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,2,\\,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}2x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Horizontal Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4f6adec-8bd5-59c7-b63f-9f590e0578a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354213} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7f6d92-ab57-518e-b9b3-0bff1b6395ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354214} {:answer/id #uuid "b3876681-feef-5515-9cd3-04ead1b06e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354215} {:answer/id #uuid "d86b2f9c-ee86-5212-87d6-895fd59a87cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354216} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa72309-0560-5c41-ab5d-6a4a38a75c7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3876681-feef-5515-9cd3-04ead1b06e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354215} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf2c485a-6c1d-5e7e-b9b6-ab88e6409ed2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "87b812e7-70f0-5b8a-863a-f8d05009cbc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-365879" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y,\\,4x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=4$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=4$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,4x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,y,\\,4x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}4x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {[2{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Horizontal Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1ef5a4d-b5be-5746-b695-eac02235aa74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354220} {:answer/id #uuid "f58fe6e2-5c8f-5be1-850a-27e8e1b00d20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354221} {:answer/id #uuid "d1439cf5-3c64-593d-9ac7-2a47568830a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354222} {:answer/id #uuid "db7bcabe-194a-5f07-bd12-33d451ce9bd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354223} {:answer/id #uuid "90ac626c-ab61-5f42-afc5-9d5f347206dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1ef5a4d-b5be-5746-b695-eac02235aa74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354220} :answer-field/id #uuid "3305e770-a298-53a6-88c6-a301ddcdf6b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb7e51d1-4895-5640-8b7f-9992fc6a947d" :question/math-academy-id "q-365880" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,x+2y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=2$ for  $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,x+2y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-(x+2y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-(x+2y))\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x+2y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-(x+2y))\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}[(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+2xy){|}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}+2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{y}{2}+{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Horizontal Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1d7b192-b321-5b96-92ba-a180f407e480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354227} {:answer/id #uuid "749a3585-b8c4-5c32-afa0-4b6dd1493299" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354228} {:answer/id #uuid "d4793f0c-2fa1-5f85-a9cb-3d861ca19f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354229} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0e74da-8bb7-51e9-9631-3daf463386eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354230} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d57543-b756-599c-b239-d23fe507bfdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1d7b192-b321-5b96-92ba-a180f407e480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354227} :answer-field/id #uuid "135e775a-c76d-5370-bd60-fe97d8047202" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee1444d0-7e94-51bd-9978-54ebc6e79393" :question/math-academy-id "q-365882" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,x,\\,3x+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=-1$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=-1$ for  $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 2,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=-1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,x,\\,3x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-(3x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-(3x+y))\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(3x+y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(-(3x+y))\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{2}[(\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+xy){|}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{2}(6+2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[6y+{y}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
